%U A330774 1843,947,51,1,1815811,15,1255,41,2479,1,46697,33,97339,45,63,1,219347,1
%N A330774 Number of n-color perfect compositions of n.
%C A330774 An n-color perfect composition of v is a composition into j types of each part j whose sequence of parts contains one composition of every positive integer less than n.
%H A330774 A. O. Munagi, <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL23/Munagi/munagi13.html">Perfect Compositions of Numbers</a>, J. Integer Seq. 23 (2020), art. 20.5.1.
%F A330774 From _Vladeta Jovovic_, Nov 08 2026: (Start)
%F A330774 Dirichlet g.f. of a(n-1): 1 + Sum_{k>=1} k!*Product_{j=0..k-1} (zeta(s-j) - 1).
%F A330774 Equivalently, a(n) = Sum_{k>=1} k!*[x^k]P_{n+1}(x), where P_1(x) = 0 and P_m(x) = x + x*Sum_{d|m, 1<d<m} P_{m/d}(d*x) for m >= 2.
%F A330774 (End)
%e A330774 a(5)=11 because v=5 has eleven n-color perfect compositions: (1,1,1,1,1), (1,2,2),(2,2,1), (1,2',2'), (2',2',1), (1,1,3), (3,1,1), (1,1,3'), (3',1,1), (1,1,3''), (3'',1,1).
%e A330774 For n = 11, P_12(x) = x + 15*x^2 + 38*x^3, hence a(11) = 1! + 15*2! + 38*3! = 259.
